 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Ddcx7CcTX_5/",
 "title": "Handling a Client Who Wants to Leave",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "This post provides advice from Glenn Rogers on how to handle a situation when your biggest client threatens to leave. The main lesson is to invite them for a meeting, understand if expectations were missed or if a competitor lured them away, and let them go freely if they still want to leave.",
 "key_points": [
  "Call your client and suggest parting with a handshake and meeting up for tea, coffee, or lunch.",
  "Ask them if there were any shortcomings from your side or unmet expectations.",
  "Explain that starting with a new vendor means building a foundation from scratch, whereas you already have an established relationship.",
  "Address the possibility of a competitor offering them the world just to win their business.",
  "Remind them of your original plan and offer extra value or complimentary work that would normally cost thousands of dollars.",
  "If they still want to leave, give them a list of steps to help them work with the new provider, and let them go with an open heart."
 ],
 "content": "CEO ANSWERS: My Biggest Client Wants Out. My biggest client is threatening to leave, so what should I do? Okay, the first thing to do, always remember that the secret lies in the art of understanding and communication, both two sides of the SAME coin. So, what you do is call your client and say, look, we've been friends for all this while, right? If we are going to have to part, why don't we part with a handshake? So, may I have you out for tea, coffee, whatever, lunch? When they come out, sit them down and say, look, if there's any shortcoming from our side, or you had expectations that we didn't reach, now would be a great time, because, you know, starting with somebody else building the foundation, we already have all this, and we're not perfect. When they tell you fix it, you're back again. Now, if it's something, very often it is this case, that there's a competitor who has reached out to them, offering them the world just to get that business, and finally what happens? They love and leave them. So, explain it to them. Say this could be it, but never mind. Now, had we stayed together, this was our plan, and you give them a few points which otherwise would cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, and make it clear to them that this would have been completely complimentary because in your growth lies our reputation. But if you still want to go ahead with somebody else, be my guest. Take this list. I'm giving it to you with an open heart. See if they are willing to walk this path with you. If they don't, come back home, because I've remembered this one thing my mother taught me: if you love something, let it go free. If it comes back, it's yours. If it doesn't, it never was. Got a question for Glenn? Drop it below. Text on screen: CEO ANSWERS: My Biggest Client Wants Out. Instagram reel/post by Glenn Rogers. Your biggest client wants to leave. What do you do? In this episode of CEO Answers, Glenn breaks down how to handle the situation without making a desperate decision. Want to learn more from Glenn? Click the link in bio. #CEOAdvice #BusinessAdvice #Entrepreneurship #BusinessGrowth #ClientManagement",
